I like this idea. It means that you'd have to carry around both water
and coal, and when you ran out of either you'd gradually shut down.
Perhaps all potions (except for oil), not just water, could be used as
sources of steam, and the effect of the potion could be absorbed by
the automaton in this way?
If an automaton ate too much coal/drank too much water, it would die
in the same way that a human chokes on food:

You consume the small lump of coal! Your engine heats up.
You consume the large lump of coal! -More-
Your engine is dangerously overheating. Stop eating? (y/n) (n)
Your engine overheats completely. The gears snap from the heat! -More-
You fall to pieces. You die. DYWYPI?

You refill your tank from the potion of water.-More-
You feel comfortably full of steam.
You refill your tank from the castle moat. -More-
You're having trouble getting all the water in. Stop filling? (y/n)
(n)
You overfill your tank. -More-
The pressure of the steam builds up. You can't hold it all in! -More-
You explode. DYWYPI?

This would mean that the endgame Riders would have to be slightly
altered for automatons (since neither Famine nor Pestilence would have
much effect on them.) Famine could be replaced by Entropy (probably
the ultimate fear of a steam-driven machine; drains the automaton of
heat each turn :-)) and Pestilence by Corrosion (has a rust/acid
attack each turn.)
Also, automatons should be probably fire-resistant, but cold would be
very bad for them because they are driven by steam. This would mean
that they are very susceptible to attacks of monsters such as winter
wolves, freezing spheres, etc. Perhaps each time they are hit with
cold, it would not lower their hit points but would instead take some
heat ("nutrition") points away, so that they are required to consume
more coal to re-heat themselves. Conversely, each hit from a steam
vortex would add some nutrition points.

